{
  "_comment": "Household equipment specs. ownership_probability, rated_power_kw, standby_power_kw for the CREST-sourced items below (see _crest_source per item) are read from the CREST Domestic Electricity Demand Model 1.0e (Richardson, Thomson, Infield -- Loughborough University CREST), obtained via the paper's own public download link; the underlying power/cycle statistics there are themselves sourced from UK Market Transformation Programme Briefing Notes (Crown Copyright). The hourly weekday/weekend weight SHAPES are this repo's own illustrative arrays (not extracted from CREST's 10-minute activity/TPM tables), calibrated so each item's expected annual triggered-use energy matches CREST's reported figure. Water heating and Electric Space Heating categories are excluded deliberately -- those are thermal loads that belong to buem's domain, not this repo's.",
  "_occupant_scaling_comment": "occupant_scaling (exponent on the gate's occupant count, see core.equipment._occupant_multiplier) is this repo's own assignment, NOT a CREST figure -- CREST's own headcount dependence lives in its 10-minute activity/TPM tables, which this repo does not reproduce. Each item is placed in one of four sharing tiers by whether an extra occupant brings their own use of it: 1.0 strictly per-person consumables (laundry loads, dishes, hot drinks, individual screen time); 0.5 partly-shared activities (one meal serves the household but grows with it); 0.3 mostly-shared (more rooms lit); 0.15 one-per-household items watched together. 1.0 is a deliberate ceiling -- above it, usage would grow faster than headcount. Before this field existed, every household item's firing probability keyed only off percent_active (a fraction, invariant in household size), so a 5-person household ran its washing machine as often as a 1-person one and annual electricity moved only 1.40x across 1-5 occupants. It now moves 2.20x, against roughly 2.75x in published NL averages by household size. The residual ~20% shortfall at 5 occupants is deliberately NOT closed by inflating these exponents past their physical meaning: it is attributed to ownership_probability being household-size-independent here (a 5-person household really is likelier to own a dishwasher, tumble dryer and second TV), which is a separate lever tracked in .claude/residential/open.md.",
